		<title type="html"><![CDATA[Re: DCS Codes - Old/New, 9600 vs 1701]]></title>

		
		<content type="html" xml:base="http://localhost/opengd77/viewtopic.php?t=5197&amp;p=37664#p37664"><![CDATA[
I know the last time I had issues with this I did a lot of research through the forums and didn't yield results beyond "these are the standard DCS codes and that's what we're sticking with." An example of functionality being added to the software that isn't supported by the hardware would be the blanket inclusion of GPS despite many radios not having that functionality. The only impact of the software including tones that aren't supported by the hardware is that the given radio may not work a repeater that's using the non-standard tones; which is no different result than the current situation but impedes radios whose hardware could.<br><br>If the radio does have the hardware to handle the wildcat tones, then according to internet research it could on occasion erroneously unsquelch thinking it's hearing the correct code, God forbid. I'd say it's up to the end user at that point to either skip it on the scan manually, or edit the channel details to control how the scan handles the channel to their liking, which is already within their ability with the existing software. I offer this thread as a case study, when an end user exploits a glitch in the CPS and the software that existed a year ago, then two different MD-9600's bought at two times work flawlessly, not even opening at random. The 1701 doesn't self-destruct, it just doesn't unsquelch for the tone it doesn't recognize. The user does get indication of activity on the channel, but has to manually monitor to see if they want to listen or not.<br><br>Are all polite edit requests met with "you're free to fix it yourself"? I appreciate that this is free and open source, and that counts for a lot; but I wouldn't have the first clue about how to proceed, and obviously spending the time learning how to edit my own copy would not update the ongoing changes that are taking place at the source. My workaround took a little time to figure out but nothing like trying to learn how to edit the source code. As far as time goes I do have an interest in this but it still took a week for me to circle back around to this and check this thread. At that rate I should be all set to make my own change in about fifteen years. I do appreciate that there are those with the willingness, time and the ability that are already deep into editing the software - and as a busy guy myself I do appreciate their efforts, completely.<br><br>I put up the details of the problem and my workaround to try to help others who could be trying to search around and deal with a similar problem in their circumstance, if nothing else.<p>Statistics: Posted by <a href="http://localhost/opengd77/memberlist.php?mode=viewprofile&amp;u=6008">K3SAD</a> — Sun Jul 06, 2025 1:57 pm</p><hr />
]]></content>
